\chapter{Introduction and Design Philosophy}

MuDSL builds Domain-Specific Languages in C++20 and D. The C++ implementation has the public header \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p}, exposing the \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l} namespace. The D entry point is \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}d}, imported with \texttt{i\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L}. Both hosts use the accompanying \texttt{m\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r} implementation for MuLL analysis and optimization. Default builds use the host language's standard library. Optional native Lua embedding adds a user-supplied Lua library dependency.

This chapter introduces the library's purpose, the design ideas that shape every feature, and the mental model you will carry through the rest of the manual. It is the only chapter that is purely conceptual. From Chapter 2 onward the manual is hands-on, with compilable examples in every section.

A Domain-Specific Language, or DSL, is a small language tuned to one problem domain. Configuration files, build rules, database queries, hardware descriptions, and mathematical expression notations are all DSLs. Each is deliberately narrow, and that narrowness is its strength.

When you build a DSL \emph{inside} C++, you build an \emph{embedded} DSL. You reuse the host language's syntax, type system, and compiler while adding a thin, expressive layer that reads almost like prose. The host language does the heavy lifting; your layer provides the vocabulary.

MuDSL exists to make that layer easy to assemble from reusable pieces. The central observation is that most embedded DSLs share the same handful of building blocks. Pipes chain transformations. Predicates compose with logical operators. Pattern dispatch tables route keys to handlers. Trees represent syntax. Rewrite passes simplify trees. Lazy expressions fuse computation.

Rather than reinvent these blocks for every project, MuDSL packages each one as an orthogonal \emph{feature}. You compose exactly the features your language needs and pay only for those you use.

The library is organised around one template. A DSL is a struct that inherits \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L}, passing itself as the first argument and the desired features as the rest:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
struct MyDSL : dsl::DSL<MyDSL, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::Operators> {
    // your domain members and methods
};
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

That single line of inheritance is the whole entry point. \texttt{M\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L} is your language class. The first template argument is the class itself — a recursive technique known as CRTP, the Curiously Recurring Template Pattern.

The D entry point injects features with a template mixin:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
import MuDSL;
struct MyDSL { mixin DSL!(Pipeline, Operators); }
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

D uses native string template parameters, operator templates, alias callables and compile-time function evaluation. Chapter 31 (Chapter 31) maps the established toolkit to D. The C++ foundations below explain the CRTP implementation used by Chapters 3–29.

Every remaining argument is a \emph{feature tag}: \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s}, and so on. Each feature injects a small set of methods, operators, or nested types into your class through a mixin. The result is a fully concrete object with compile-time polymorphism and no virtual functions.

The features MuDSL ships are deliberately broad, so that one toolkit can serve many kinds of language. The pipeline feature adds the pipe operator so values flow through a chain. The operators feature gives you composable predicates joined by \texttt{\&}, \texttt{|}, and \texttt{!}.

The pattern-match feature builds compile-time dispatch tables from \texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} and \texttt{o\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e} clauses. The AST and rewrite features provide value-type syntax trees and the passes that simplify them. The expression-templates feature defers arithmetic into lazy trees that fuse on evaluation.

The custom-literals feature registers string suffixes. Several supporting utilities round out the toolkit: memoization, lazy values, monadic optionals, a \texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}t} type, parser combinators, task pipelines, and a Parsing Expression Grammar engine.

Both implementations also provide a shared S-expression substrate, e-graphs and equality saturation, user codec/\allowbreak{}I/\allowbreak{}O protocols, AST-derived visitors, static byte regular expressions and binary streams, preprocessing with optional Lua hooks, and explicit context-free grammars. These facilities compose into a source-to-AST-to-optimized-representation pipeline. Chapter 30 introduces them; Chapters 32–36 provide focused tutorials.

Because every feature is a mixin, features combine without interfering. Adding \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e} to a class does not change how \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s} behaves, and neither affects \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T}. You can mix two features or ten.

This orthogonality is a direct consequence of the design. It is one of the reasons the library stays small. It is also the reason the manual can devote one chapter to each feature without overlap.

Let us look at the smallest possible DSL. The following class inherits two features but adds no members of its own:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"

struct BasicDSL : dsl::DSL<BasicDSL, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::Operators> {};
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Even with an empty body, \texttt{B\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L} already has real behaviour. The pipeline feature contributes a static \texttt{w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p} method that lifts a value into a chain.

The operators feature contributes a static \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d} factory. The pipe operator itself is a free function in the \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l} namespace, so it works on any value paired with a \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e} stage.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto v = BasicDSL::wrap(5)
       | dsl::pipe([](int x) { return x + 3; })
       | dsl::pipe([](int x) { return x * 2; });
// v == 16
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Predicates compose with the logical operators you already know:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto gt5  = BasicDSL::make_pred([](int x) { return x > 5; });
auto even = BasicDSL::make_pred([](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; });
auto rule = gt5 & even;
// rule(16) == true, rule(7) == false
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

That example is the entire \texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p} program. It exercises three ideas at once: feature composition, the pipe chain, and predicate algebra. The rest of the manual unpacks each in depth.

\section{The Eight Design Pillars}

The first design pillar is \emph{static polymorphism through CRTP}. Traditional object-oriented polymorphism uses virtual functions. A base class declares a virtual method, derived classes override it, and a call through a base pointer dispatches at run time through a vtable.

That dispatch costs an indirection and defeats inlining. CRTP instead lets the base class know the derived type at compile time. The base casts \texttt{*\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s} to the derived type, and the compiler sees a concrete call it can inline completely.

MuDSL uses this everywhere, so there is no virtual call anywhere in the toolkit. The base class is short and worth quoting in full:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
template <typename Derived, typename... Features>
struct DSL : Features::template Mixin<Derived>... {
protected:
  constexpr Derived &self() noexcept {
    return static_cast<Derived &>(*this);
  }
  constexpr const Derived &self() const noexcept {
    return static_cast<const Derived &>(*this);
  }
};
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The second pillar is \emph{feature mixins through multiple inheritance}. Each feature is a struct with a nested \texttt{M\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}>} template. \texttt{D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L} inherits from every feature's mixin at once, so the derived class gains the union of all their methods.

Because each mixin is parametrised on \texttt{D\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d}, those methods can reach back into your class when needed. The rewrite feature calls \texttt{D\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s}; the literals feature reads \texttt{D\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s}. The contract between a feature and the class that hosts it is therefore small and explicit.

The third pillar is \emph{non-type template parameters carrying strings}. Before C++20, you could not pass a string literal as a template argument directly. MuDSL defines a small \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}>} type that wraps a compile-time char array and qualifies as a non-type template parameter.

This lets you write \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}4\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{})} and \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>}. The tags and patterns live in the type system, enabling \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} matching with no run-time parsing of the string. Chapter 4 is devoted to \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}.

The fourth pillar is \emph{value semantics}. The library's data structures — \texttt{A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{M\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}>}, \texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}>}, the expression-tree nodes — are value types. They are copyable, movable, and stack-friendly.

You can return them from functions, store them in containers, and pass them by value without thinking about ownership. Where heap is used it is hidden behind a value interface, as with \texttt{A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e}'s internal vector of children.

The fifth pillar is \emph{deferred evaluation}. Both \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}>} and the expression templates postpone work. \texttt{L\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}>} runs a producer exactly once on first access and caches the result.

Expression templates build a tree of operations as you write \texttt{a\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}*\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c} and perform a single fused evaluation when you call \texttt{.\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})}. Deferral lets you describe computation cheaply and commit to it deliberately.

The sixth pillar is \emph{explicit error flow}. MuDSL does not favour exceptions for control flow. The \texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}>} type models success and failure as data, with \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p}, \texttt{a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n}, and \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}r} to transform each branch.

The parser combinator core returns an \texttt{E\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}>} that carries an optional value \emph{and} a structured \texttt{P\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r}. A failed parse is not an exception but a value you inspect.

The seventh pillar is \emph{compositionality through operators}. C++ lets you overload operators, and MuDSL leans on this to make DSLs read naturally.

The pipe \texttt{|}, the logical \texttt{\&\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}|\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}!} on predicates, the arithmetic \texttt{+\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}*\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}} on expression-templated values, and the \texttt{\&\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}|\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}*} on parsers all build larger abstractions from smaller ones using ordinary infix syntax. Operators are the connective tissue of the library, not a gimmick.

The eighth pillar is \emph{additive growth}. The most recent part of the toolkit, the Parsing Expression Grammar subsystem, was layered on top of the existing pattern-matching and parser-combinator machinery without modifying any earlier type.

The library is structured so that new subsystems extend rather than reshape what came before. This matters to you as a user because the documented behaviour of an old feature will not quietly change when a new one arrives.

\section{What the Pillars Buy You}

These eight pillars are not abstract. They are the reason a program against MuDSL is short, fast, and type-safe at the same time.

CRTP and mixins give you composition \emph{and} zero overhead together. These are two goals that usually conflict; here they are reconciled by doing all the work at compile time.

NTTP strings give you readable, compiler-checked tags \emph{and} run-time performance, because the string never has to be re-parsed at run time. Value semantics give you safety \emph{and} simplicity, because there are no dangling pointers to reason about.

\section{Who MuDSL Is For}

MuDSL is for library authors who want to ship a small embedded language without pulling in a framework. It is for application developers who need a configuration parser, a query builder, a rule engine, or a state machine without leaving C++.

It is for compiler and tooling writers who need a parser combinator core and a rewrite engine for ASTs. And it is for anyone who wants to see how modern C++ — concepts, NTTPs, \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r}, variadic templates — comes together to build something practical.

\section{What MuDSL Is Not}

It is equally important to know what MuDSL is \emph{not}. It is not a full general-purpose parser generator like ANTLR. The PEG and combinator subsystems are intentionally lightweight.

It is not a regex library. The \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} engine implements a small, fixed subset of regular-expression syntax. It is not a threading library; the caching and lazy helpers are deliberately single-threaded.

And it is not a replacement for the standard library's optionals and variants. Rather, it composes with them, wrapping \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l} in \texttt{M\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}e} and building \texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}t} on top of \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t}.

\section{Requirements}

The requirements are minimal and worth stating up front. You need a compiler that implements C++20. The library uses concepts, class-type non-type template parameters, \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} lambdas, and modern standard-library idioms.

You need no external dependencies. Everything comes from the standard library headers that \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p} itself includes. And because the library is header-only, there is nothing to link.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"   // that is the whole dependency
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The distribution provides a CMake \texttt{I\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}E} library target so that projects using CMake can consume MuDSL the idiomatic way. The target sets \texttt{c\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}0} as a required compile feature and exports the header into an include directory.

Chapter 2 walks through both the CMake route and the plain-compiler route in detail, including how to install the package and find it downstream.

