\section{The Mental Model}

The mental model to carry forward is simple. A MuDSL program is a C++ program in which you do three things.

First, you define a struct that inherits \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L} with the features you want. Second, you add whatever domain-specific members or methods you need. Third, you express the language's logic using the operators and helpers those features provide.

Everything else — the trees, the parsers, the pipes, the caches — is machinery in service of that three-step loop. The loop is the same whether your language is a state machine, a query builder, or a vector algebra.

\section{A Dispatch-Driven Example}

Consider how the loop plays out for a state-machine language. You want states and events with transitions driven by pattern matching.

You inherit both \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e} and \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h}. You store a current state as a member. You build a transition table with \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h}. The features hand you the pieces; you write the domain.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
struct SM : dsl::DSL<SM, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::PatternMatch> {
    enum class S { Idle, Running, Done };
    static constexpr auto transitions = dsl::match(
        dsl::when<S::Idle>   ([](auto e){ return e=="start" ? S::Running : S::Idle; }),
        dsl::when<S::Running>([](auto e){ return e=="stop"  ? S::Done    : S::Running; }),
        dsl::otherwise       ([](auto){ return S::Done; })
    );
    S state = S::Idle;
    void send(std::string_view e){ state = transitions(state, e); }
};
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Sending events drives the state forward. After \texttt{s\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{})} the state is \texttt{R\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}; after \texttt{s\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{})} it is \texttt{D\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e}. Two features and a handful of lines implement a complete transition system.

\section{An Arithmetic Example}

Or consider a vector arithmetic language built on expression templates. You inherit \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s}, hold a vector of floats, and expose an \texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} accessor.

The arithmetic operators are then supplied by the mixin, and evaluation fuses into a single pass over the data.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
struct VecDSL : dsl::DSL<VecDSL, dsl::ExprTemplates> {
    std::vector<float> data;
    const std::vector<float>& expr_value() const { return data; }
};
// VecDSL a{{1,2,3}}, b{{4,5,6}};
// auto result = (a + b).eval();  // {5, 7, 9}
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

These two examples — one dispatch-driven, one arithmetic-driven — show the range of languages MuDSL supports from the same base class. The features differ, but the loop is identical.

\section{How the Manual Is Organised}

The remainder of the manual is a systematic tour, organised into twelve parts. Part I finishes the foundations: how to obtain and build the library, how CRTP and mixins work, how \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} enables string template parameters, and how the feature tags and utility concepts are defined.

Parts II through XII then take each subsystem in turn. You will learn the pipe and predicate algebras, the pattern matcher and its regex engine, the AST and its rewrites, the expression templates, the literals, the caching and lazy helpers, the monadic and result types, the parser combinator core, the task pipeline, and finally the PEG grammar engine with its matcher.

Each chapter is self-contained enough to be read in isolation. At the same time, chapters cross-reference their neighbours, so a reader who goes in order builds a connected picture rather than a bag of tricks.

\section{A Note on the Code Listings}

Every snippet in this manual uses the real \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:} API and is written to compile against \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p} with a conforming C++20 compiler. Where a snippet is abbreviated for space — for instance, omitting \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e} lines or \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n} — that is noted.

The companion \texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}} directory in the source distribution contains complete, compilable programs for nearly every feature. This manual frequently echoes and expands on those examples, so you can read prose here and run code there.

\section{A Brief Glossary}

A few terms recur throughout the manual and are worth defining once. A \emph{feature tag} is one of the empty structs like \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e} that you pass to \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L}.

A \emph{mixin} is the nested \texttt{M\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}>} template inside a feature tag; it is where the feature's methods actually live. A \emph{clause} is one arm of a \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h} table, built with \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n} or \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e}.

An \emph{NTTP} is a non-type template parameter — a value, as opposed to a type, passed between angle brackets. \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>} and \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>} both use NTTPs. A \emph{fixpoint} is the state a rewrite pass reaches when no rule fires any more; Chapter 14 covers this in detail.

\section{Expectations and Scope}

MuDSL values clarity and composability above completeness. The pattern engine implements a useful subset, not full POSIX regex. The parser combinators are a teaching-quality Parsec, not an industrial parser toolkit. The PEG engine is additive and lightweight.

Within its chosen scope, however, each piece is consistent, documented, and designed to combine with the others. Understanding the design philosophy in this chapter will make the concrete APIs in later chapters feel predictable rather than arbitrary.

\section{Summary}

MuDSL is a header-only C++20 toolkit for embedded DSLs, built on CRTP, feature mixins, and NTTP strings. You compose features by inheriting \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L}, and the compiler does all the polymorphic work. The library favours value semantics, deferred evaluation, explicit error flow, and operator-based composition, and it grows additively rather than by rewriting itself.

The next chapter turns philosophy into practice. You will obtain the header, compile a first program with and without CMake, run the bundled examples, and learn to read the most common compile errors. From there, Chapter 3 opens the \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L} base class and shows exactly how one line of inheritance becomes a fully featured language.
